WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:04.004
Growing up in India, the sheer poverty

00:00:04.004 --> 00:00:06.172
and inequality that I'd seen made me very interested

00:00:06.172 --> 00:00:07.674
in trying to understand

00:00:07.674 --> 00:00:10.135
what economics could do

00:00:10.135 --> 00:00:12.679
and I think what I discovered during my PhD was that

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:14.848
you could use the tools of economics

00:00:14.848 --> 00:00:18.184
to actually ask much more nuanced questions about

00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:19.310
power and politics.

00:00:20.437 --> 00:00:22.480
Why was it that some institutions

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.983
would systematically exclude certain groups

00:00:24.983 --> 00:00:26.735
and what it would take to give

00:00:26.735 --> 00:00:28.486
historically disadvantaged groups

00:00:28.486 --> 00:00:31.197
political and economic power.

00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:32.198
I'm Rohini Pande,

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:34.325
Professor of Economics at Yale University

00:00:34.325 --> 00:00:36.494
And Director of the Economic Growth Center.

00:00:37.996 --> 00:00:40.040
Central to economics is this idea

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.041
that there are tradeoffs

00:00:41.041 --> 00:00:43.251
because we have a finite set of resources

00:00:43.251 --> 00:00:47.255
and we have to decide how to allocate them.

00:00:47.255 --> 00:00:49.549
Do we do it through markets and price mechanisms?

00:00:49.549 --> 00:00:51.217
Do we do it through redistribution

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:52.719
through the government?

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:54.971
In my own work, I'm very interested

00:00:54.971 --> 00:00:57.432
in thinking about policy as a tool of change.

00:01:05.398 --> 00:01:08.359
How can we look at patterns in the world

00:01:08.359 --> 00:01:09.569
to try to understand

00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:11.988
what policies will increase equality,

00:01:11.988 --> 00:01:15.116
what policies will lead to trade offs between

00:01:15.116 --> 00:01:17.577
economic well-being and social justice?

00:01:17.577 --> 00:01:19.662
And I think that's the power of economics

00:01:19.662 --> 00:01:20.580
when it's well done,

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.208
is actually being able to quantify those tradeoffs

00:01:38.348 --> 00:01:41.351
And repeatedly, what we heard was this concern that

00:01:41.351 --> 00:01:43.561
if a woman or a young girl had a phone,

00:01:43.561 --> 00:01:44.854
she would get a boyfriend.

00:01:44.854 --> 00:01:46.898
And it seemed that there was something

00:01:46.898 --> 00:01:48.399
linked between very much

00:01:48.399 --> 00:01:49.400
based on the caste system,

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:50.860
these notions of purity

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:52.821
and controlling phone use of women.

00:02:01.121 --> 00:02:04.124
We go to the field and then really talk to them

00:02:04.124 --> 00:02:05.458
through a set of questions

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:08.545
to understand their economic lives, their social lives,

00:02:08.545 --> 00:02:10.755
their preferences, their behavior.

00:02:18.847 --> 00:02:20.140
We typically end up surveying

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:21.891
both a woman and her husband

00:02:21.891 --> 00:02:22.892
because one of the things

00:02:22.892 --> 00:02:24.602
that we're interested in is also looking at

00:02:24.602 --> 00:02:26.104
whether on similar questions

00:02:26.104 --> 00:02:28.606
they may have very different responses.

00:02:28.606 --> 00:02:30.525
A women might think that a mobile phone

00:02:30.525 --> 00:02:33.278
is appropriate for a woman to use,

00:02:33.278 --> 00:02:35.071
but her husband may not think it is.

00:02:35.071 --> 00:02:37.157
And that discord may be very informative

00:02:37.157 --> 00:02:39.659
of what actually happens.

00:02:43.955 --> 00:02:46.875
Certainly, I think a central part of economics

00:02:46.875 --> 00:02:48.626
is the toolkit

00:02:48.626 --> 00:02:50.920
is the way of thinking about abstraction

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:53.047
of how do you go from

00:02:53.047 --> 00:02:54.007
just seeing everything

00:02:54.007 --> 00:02:54.883
that's happening in a world

00:02:54.883 --> 00:02:57.343
to distill it down to one force

00:02:57.343 --> 00:02:58.386
that you can focus on

00:02:58.386 --> 00:02:59.721
and say that was something

00:02:59.721 --> 00:03:00.972
that affected behavior.

00:03:00.972 --> 00:03:03.725
And I think that's something economics is very good at.

00:03:03.725 --> 00:03:04.809
But I think it's dangerous

00:03:04.809 --> 00:03:06.102
to take it to the next step

00:03:06.102 --> 00:03:07.645
of saying that as a result,

00:03:07.645 --> 00:03:09.355
we know something about what's

00:03:09.355 --> 00:03:11.357
going to make people wealthier

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:13.151
or happier.

00:03:13.151 --> 00:03:14.319
I think to understand that,

00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:16.070
you have to have a much more nuanced view

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:18.531
of politics, of power.

00:03:18.531 --> 00:03:20.408
Economics can get to that

00:03:20.408 --> 00:03:21.784
but I think that's where a little bit

00:03:21.784 --> 00:03:24.954
more humility and learning from other

00:03:24.954 --> 00:03:26.706
disciplines is valuable.

00:03:26.706 --> 00:03:28.416
Economics is also, I think,

00:03:28.416 --> 00:03:29.500
being pushed in the right direction

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:30.793
by a lot of the social movements

00:03:30.793 --> 00:03:31.628
that we are seeing around

00:03:31.628 --> 00:03:33.630
issues of inclusion, of justice.

00:03:34.756 --> 00:03:36.382
Economists have to think hard about

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:39.260
how do we actually think of notions of justice,

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:40.970
not just efficiency.

